pection as a result a lot of these  films deal with identity and stories  about private lives American beauty is  about the inner lives of suburban  neighbors while the setting of the film  represents the idyllic American  landscape of the time the characters  represent the anxieties underlying that  pristine exterior both the families have  unfulfilled home lives the father is a  borderline sexual predator and the  neighbor is a secret white supremacist  who also happens to have repressed  homosexual feelings this movie  criticizes the perceived stability of  the era suggesting that it's simply a  mask that hides the true self the  flowers in the garden the couch and the  90s lifestyle are not true beauty but  rather mimicking beauty it's an  artificial American Beauty Fight Club  takes this opportunity for introspection  and turns it into a conversation about  manhood a world without conflict means a  world where men don't have a way to  assert their own masculinity so the  characters go to great lengths to  manufacture conflict and chaos to  satisfy their masculine impulses the  movie satirizes this way of thinking as  an ineffective and unproductive way to  find purpose even though the film was  intended as a cautionary tale many men  took it a bit too literally maybe the  satire was too relevant really the movie  reminds us that the stability and  uneventful nosov the 90s was largely a  middle-class white male phenomenon film  starring minorities or women like boys  don't cry and all about my mother show  us that for some people the 90s were  anything but boring and stable these  movies highlight the issues still  present underneath that stable suburban  exterior cubicle movies star men who  have so little conflict in their life  that they seek it out and they can only  really do so because they have the time  and the money and there's no movie from  1999 that expresses the ideas of the  cubicle movie more than being John  Malkovich the film exaggerates the  oppressive corporate imagery in films  like office space by creating an absurd  office with low ceilings here the office  literally bears down on its employees  when Craig discovers a portal that lets  you live in John Malkovich his mind for  15 minutes he uses it to escape his  boring life  he even monetizes the portal and it has  massive appeal to people who are tired  of their boring lives and want to mimic  the seemingly exciting life of a  celebrity  Craig uses Malkovich's status for sexual  gratification and career goals but these  shallow desires never lead him to true  happiness living the life of someone  else means he never worked to make his  own life better he tried to escape his  problems by mimicking someone else  instead of exploring what was within  himself Charlie Kaufman said he wrote  the film while he worked as a writer on  the TV show get a life the best part of  the show came from the scripts written  by its original creators so all the  other writers tried to imitate their  voice the results he said was extremely  unfulfilled not only because it wasn't  personal work but because mimicking  someone else inherently means that you  are not interesting enough Bing John  Malkovich and to an extent all cubicle  movies seem to reflect this idea in some  way social forces compel us to conform  ourselves into a certain definition of  success like having the good job and a  comfortable life but having to do this  requires the belief that who we already  are isn't good enough  I think cubical movies are trying to  remind us that we are worth it the point  is there doesn't have to be some crisis  going on for a movie to reflect world  events 1999 had this crazy phenomenon  where nearly a dozen major Hollywood  releases feature a main character tired  of the stability monotony and uneventful  nosov their life and it's a perfect  mirror of what America was like in the  late 90s the movies of that year  reminded people to reclaim their  confidence and search for a more  exciting and personal future in other  words they reminded us to party like  it's 1999  [Music]  to truly understand the production of a  movie you also have to understand the  time period it was made in another  classic example of history influencing  film is German expressionist cinema of  the 1920s not only should you watch some  of those movies but to understand them  better I'd also recommend you learn  about the impact of World War one on  Germany which greatly altered their  society and in turn their movies you can  learn more about that time in the  secrets of World War one documentary  available on curiosity stream curiosity  stream has recently expanded their  library of documentaries to more than  2400 titles about everything from  history to technology these high-quality  videos are a great way to broaden your  understanding of the world around us go  to curiosity stream comm slash now you  see it for unlimited access to the  world's top documentaries and nonfiction  series enter the promo code now you see  it when prompted during the signup  process and your membership is  completely free for the first 30 days  thanks for watching and thank you to my  patreon supporters who all got early  access to this video before the general  public
 

